---
import { readFileSync } from "node:fs";
import { resolve } from "node:path";
import Page from "../layouts/Page.astro";
import Excluded from "../components/Excluded.astro";
import { gtBuild, mappingCheck, fmt, href, repoUrl } from "../lib/data";
const mc = mappingCheck();

const prompt = (f: string) => readFileSync(resolve(process.cwd(), "../src/snapme_eval/prompts", f), "utf8").trim();
const gt = gtBuild();
const c = gt?.counts ?? {};
---
<Page title="Methods" description="Photos, model requests, food coding and measures used in the SNAPMe benchmark.">
  <h1>Methods</h1>
  <p class="lede">This benchmark compares food lists estimated from photos with participants' ASA24 food records.
    It measures agreement in food identification, preparation and grams. The reference describes reported intake;
    it is not a weighed food record.</p>

  <h2 id="data">1. Study data</h2>
  <p>SNAPMe DB v1.0 contains phone photos and ASA24-2020 food records from {c.participants ?? 95} adults over{" "}
    {c.participant_days ?? 275} study days. Participants recorded up to three days each. They passed a screening
    test on foods and portion sizes and received instruction in recording their diet. The study protocol is
    described by <a href="https://doi.org/10.3390/nu15234972">Larke et al. (2023)</a>.</p>
  <p>For non-packaged foods, participants were asked to photograph each plate, bowl or cup before and after eating,
    at about 45 degrees and 18 inches from the food. A 1.5 x 1.5 inch checkerboard marker at the lower left provided
    a size reference. Single-serving packaged foods were photographed from the front and at the ingredient label.
    Foods served from larger packages followed the before-and-after procedure.</p>
  <p>Participants entered foods and amounts eaten in ASA24's food-record mode on the same study day. Study staff
    reviewed the photos and food records and linked ASA24 lines to photos. The protocol excluded water, chewing gum
    and seasonings; the model prompts also request their omission. A dish can occupy several ASA24 lines, including
    additions such as milk in coffee. Lines sharing a FoodNum within a photo form one dish in this benchmark.</p>

  <h2 id="reference">2. Reference records</h2>
  <p>Each evaluation uses one before photo or one package-front photo and its linked ASA24 lines. The reference
    build contains {fmt.n(c.images ?? 1675)} photos: {fmt.n(c.images_by_type?.before ?? 1473)} before photos and{" "}
    {fmt.n(c.images_by_type?.front ?? 202)} package fronts. The linkage file has {fmt.n(c.linked_rows ?? 4733)} lines
    with a photo filename and {fmt.n(c.unlinked_rows ?? 28)} without one. One named photo is missing from the
    release; it and its linked line are excluded. The remaining records contain{" "}
    {fmt.n(c.unique_food_codes ?? 1151)} distinct food codes.</p>
  <p>Each retained ASA24 line supplies a food description, code and grams eaten. Codes in FNDDS 2017-2018 retain
    their What We Eat in America (WWEIA) categories. For an eight-digit ASA24 code absent from FNDDS, the code
    prefix is used to assign the most common WWEIA category among sufficiently similar FNDDS codes. Recipe
    ingredients recorded with shorter SR Legacy codes have no WWEIA category or FNDDS subgroup; a major food group
    is assigned where the SR group has a defined correspondence. These ingredients remain in the reference.</p>
  <p>Photos are grouped by the number of distinct codes in their record: 1, 2-3, or 4 or more, following the
    grouping in Figure 3 of the paper. The <a href={href("data/gt_build.json")}>reference build record</a> gives
    counts, source checksums and exclusions. Category assignment is implemented in{" "}
    <a href={repoUrl("src/snapme_eval/gt/snapme.py")}>gt/snapme.py</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="tasks">3. Photo inputs and prompts</h2>
  <p>A prompt is the written request sent to a model with a photo. Models receive the same prompt for a given
    method and photo type. Photos are rotated according to their file metadata, converted to JPEG and reduced
    when necessary to at most 1,536 pixels on the long side. The participant-based development split contains{" "}
    {fmt.n(c.images_by_split?.dev ?? 165)} photos. Reported full-set scores include these photos.</p>
  <p>The first method asks for foods, drinks, preparation, visible ingredients of mixed dishes and grams from a
    before photo or package front. Four further methods change the information available:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>The before-and-after method adds the after photo, or the ingredient label for a packaged food, and asks for
      the amount eaten. If that second photo is missing, the request uses the first photo alone.</li>
    <li>The combined-list method gives a model the photo and food lists from several earlier runs. It asks for one
      list based on the photo. A missing list is omitted for that photo.</li>
    <li>The volume-assisted method first asks a model to mark each food with a rectangle. MoGe-2 estimates
      three-dimensional positions from the photo. The code fits a table plane and estimates volume above a local
      base near each rectangle's edge. The marker sets the scale when a usable marker estimate is available;
      otherwise the MoGe-2 scale is retained. The model receives these volume estimates in a second request for
      grams. Package fronts and photos without usable geometry use the first method.</li>
    <li>The food-checking method uses a second model to review each item from an earlier run. It returns
      probabilities for yes/no and multiple-choice answers: whether the food is present, which FNDDS food fits,
      and which household portion fits. Foods with a presence probability below 0.5 are omitted.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The before-and-after comparison subtracts the same model's one-photo result from its two-photo result,
    using the same photos with an after photo. The photos support an estimate of the amount eaten; they do not
    provide weighed intake. ASA24 describes grams eaten, while a before photo shows grams served.</p>
  <p>The scale-card comparison uses meal photos where the card was located in replies from the two geometry
    runs and confirmed by a pixel test for the checkerboard pattern. The source set contains 1,473 meal photos.
    Four conditions are compared with the standard run on the same photos: the card painted over and its
    instruction removed; the card painted over with standard instructions; the photo unchanged with the card
    instruction removed; and a patch of the same size painted over elsewhere while the card remains visible.
    The last condition checks the effect of painting over part of the photo. Each reported difference is the
    changed condition minus the standard run.</p>
  <p>Food checking uses up to 15 FNDDS candidates from the name-search cache, plus "none of these foods".
    If "none" receives the highest probability, the nearest candidate is retained. Portion options are multiples
    of up to five FNDDS household measures, chosen near the earlier estimate of grams. The final grams are their
    probability-weighted mean. Where no usable food or portion choices are available, the earlier name or grams
    are retained. Source: <a href={repoUrl("src/snapme_eval/systems/check.py")}>check.py</a>.</p>
  <p>The exact prompts are reproduced below. Their wording is part of the benchmark protocol.</p>
  <details><summary>Before photo</summary><pre>{prompt("direct_v2_before.txt")}</pre></details>
  <details><summary>Package front</summary><pre>{prompt("direct_v2_front.txt")}</pre></details>
  <details><summary>Before and after photos</summary><pre>{prompt("consumed_v2_before.txt")}</pre></details>
  <details><summary>Package front and ingredient label</summary><pre>{prompt("consumed_v2_front.txt")}</pre></details>
  <details><summary>Combining food lists</summary><pre>{prompt("combine_v2.txt")}</pre></details>
  <details><summary>Locating foods for volume estimation</summary><pre>{prompt("geometry_v2_locate.txt")}</pre></details>
  <details><summary>Estimating grams with volume estimates</summary><pre>{prompt("geometry_v2_amounts.txt")}</pre></details>
  <details><summary>Nutrition5k overhead photo</summary><pre>{prompt("direct_v2_overhead.txt")}</pre></details>
  <p>Volume calculations: <a href={repoUrl("src/snapme_eval/geometry.py")}>geometry.py</a>.
    All methods: <a href={repoUrl("docs/pipelines.md")}>method implementation guide</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="models">4. Models and run settings</h2>
  <p>Vision-language models accept images and text. Most models here write a food list; the food-checking models
    return probabilities for specified answers. The models are open-weight: their numerical parameters are
    available to download under the developer's license. Each file in{" "}
    <a href={repoUrl("configs/models")}>configs/models</a> records the model's source, fixed revision, license,
    parameter count, model weight format and run settings.</p>
  <p>Model weight format describes how the numerical parameters are stored. BF16 uses a 16-bit floating-point
    format; FP8 uses 8 bits; INT4 uses 4-bit integers; NVFP4 uses a 4-bit floating-point format with scaling.
    Fewer bits reduce model weight storage. The Results page compares developer-published versions of the same model
    where both have completed runs.</p>
  <p>Parameter counts are in billions and come from the model configurations. A mixture-of-experts model selects
    groups of parameters, called experts, for each text token (a word or part of a word). "Active" describes
    the language-model parameter count per token. The registry uses the developer's stated active count when
    available; otherwise it uses a count from the downloaded model weight files. When neither supplies a count,
    the table says "active count unavailable".</p>
  <details><summary>How parameter counts are calculated</summary>
    <p>The <a href={repoUrl("tools/param_count.py")}>counting tool</a> includes shared language-model parameters
      and shared experts in full. For experts selected separately at each layer, it counts the fraction
      selected per token. Parts that process images or audio, layers that predict several tokens at once,
      and per-layer lookup tables for tokens or token sequences are included in the total but excluded from
      the calculated active count. Extra values used to scale low-precision model weights are excluded from both counts. Packing
      two 4-bit model weights into a byte does not halve the parameter count.</p>
    <p>The calculation leaves the active count unavailable if the routing settings are missing, expert banks
      are shared across layers, or expert counts differ by layer. The{" "}
      <a href={repoUrl("docs/add_model.md")}>model-registration guide</a> describes how the counts are checked.</p>
  </details>
  <p>The run software is vLLM, SGLang or LMDeploy, or the registered Python server for a decision model.
    Versions and model revisions are recorded. Text generation uses temperature 0, which selects the most likely
    next token. The usual output limit is 4,096 tokens, increased to at least 12,288 for
    models that always include a reasoning step. Optional reasoning is disabled; model-specific settings are
    recorded in the registry. Hardware and software changes can still change answers at temperature 0.</p>
  <p>The reported intervals do not measure variation between repeated runs. Allocations of graphics processors (GPUs) and engine settings
    are recorded with each run. The cluster scripts are in <a href={repoUrl("hpc/atlas")}>hpc/atlas</a>.</p>
  <p>Model runs used GPU nodes of Atlas, the SCINet high-performance computing cluster hosted at Mississippi
    State University under an agreement with USDA ARS, with NVIDIA A100 80 GB and L40S 48 GB graphics processors.</p>
  <Excluded />

  <h2 id="names">5. Supplemental food-name coding</h2>
  <p>For the supplemental code-based measures, model food names are assigned FNDDS 2017-2018 codes. Qwen3-Embedding-8B represents
    each name and FNDDS description as a vector (a list of numbers), then retrieves the 20 descriptions most similar to the
    name. Qwen3.8-27B reads the name and candidates and selects one food or no match. This step uses text only:
    neither the photo nor its ASA24 codes are supplied.</p>
  <p>Codes are stored by normalized name, with case and repeated spaces removed. A stored name receives the same
    code in later runs. The food-checking method supplies its own selected code; other methods use this shared
    name-to-code table. Coding errors can affect both food-code and category scores.</p>
  {mc && <p>The coding check compares mapped ASA24 descriptions with their recorded codes. Among the{" "}
    {fmt.n(mc.lines_fndds_code)} checked lines whose recorded code is in FNDDS, {fmt.pct(mc.same_code_share)}{" "}
    received that code. Across all {fmt.n(mc.lines)} checked lines, {fmt.pct(mc.same_wweia_share)} received the
    recorded WWEIA category. This check uses descriptions already in the mapping cache. It does not establish
    accuracy for names written by models.</p>}
  <p>Code: <a href={repoUrl("src/snapme_eval/mapping.py")}>mapping.py</a> and{" "}
    <a href={repoUrl("src/snapme_eval/score/mapcheck.py")}>mapcheck.py</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="measures">6. Measures</h2>
  <p>The main SNAPMe measures use pairs assigned by the text model gpt-oss-120b, which has open model weights.
    Its developer has no model in the benchmark tables. The grader, <code>align_v1</code>, receives one request
    per photo list: numbered ASA24 food descriptions and lettered model food names with their listed ingredients.
    It receives no photo or food code. Requests use temperature 0 and medium reasoning effort.</p>
  <p>The grader assigns each pair one of three grades:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Same food:</b> the entries name the same food, including different names for it. Details a photo cannot
      show do not lower this grade: added cooking fat, fresh/frozen/canned origin, milk fat level, salt, brand,
      sweetening unless visible, and "NS"/"NFS" wording. Cut or form, raw versus cooked, and cooking method are
      also ignored unless they distinguish different foods. A specific listed food can match a general record
      line as the same food.</li>
    <li><b>Same kind:</b> the entries name different foods of the same kind, or the list gives a general name
      where the record identifies a specific food. This grade is stored as <code>type</code>.</li>
    <li><b>Part of a mixed dish:</b> one entry is a component of the mixed dish named by the other. Parts can
      be paired in either direction, including recorded oils, sauces and seasonings. This grade is stored as{" "}
      <code>part</code>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Different foods are left unpaired. A beverage and its additions are separate foods. Scoring assigns
    same-food pairs first, then same-kind pairs, using each list entry and record line at most once across
    those pairs. Part pairs are retained as returned by the grader and may share list entries or record lines.</p>
  <p>"Recorded foods found" is the share of ASA24 lines in a pair. "Listed foods in the record" is the share
    of model list entries in a pair. Both are calculated at two levels: same food, and same food or kind.
    Parts of mixed dishes count at both levels. F1 is the harmonic mean of the two shares, so a high value
    requires both to be high. Each measure is calculated per photo, then averaged across photos.</p>
  <p>Food-list corrections per photo are the sum of recorded lines with no pair (add), listed entries with no
    pair (remove), and one-to-one pairs graded as the same kind (rename). "Photos needing no correction" is the
    share with a zero count. Amount corrections are counted separately on same-food pairs: estimates more than
    25% from the recorded grams. Raw or cooked agreement uses same-food pairs whose recorded description says
    raw or names a cooking method.</p>
  <p>Graded amount measures use same-food pairs with at least 10 g recorded and a positive estimate. Foods and
    drinks are reported separately; drinks use the WWEIA milk and beverage categories. The measures are the
    share within 25% of recorded grams, the typical factor, and bias. The typical factor is the exponential of
    the median absolute log ratio of estimated to recorded grams. It expresses disagreement as a multiplier,
    with values nearer one indicating closer estimates. Bias uses the median signed log ratio, converted back
    to a ratio and expressed as a percentage above or below the record. Negative bias means estimates below
    recorded grams. Runs with fewer than 99.5% of their photos graded appear only in the supplemental food-code table.</p>
  <p>The supplemental food-identification measures compare exact FNDDS food codes and broader WWEIA
    category, FNDDS subgroup and major-group levels. For each photo and level, precision is the share of predicted
    items matching any recorded line. Recall is the share of recorded dishes with at least one matching item.
    F1 combines the two as 2 x precision x recall / (precision + recall); it is zero when both are zero. The
    reported value is the mean F1 across photos, on a scale from 0 to 1.</p>
  <p>For the supplemental matched-item measures, predictions are paired one-to-one with ASA24 lines, first by exact food
    code and then by WWEIA category. Each prediction and each recorded line can be used once.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>ASA24 lines matched: the share of recorded lines paired with a prediction, averaged across photos.</li>
    <li>Preparation agreement: among matched lines with a recognized preparation word in the recorded
      description, the share for which the predicted name or preparation contains at least one of the same words.</li>
    <li>Token F1: agreement between sets of words after punctuation handling, removal of the paper's stopwords,
      and reduction to base forms, such as "blueberries" to "blueberry". The description measure uses predicted
      food names. The 2023 comparison also includes predicted ingredients and uses the authors' published
      per-photo reference sets from the Inverse Cooking analysis.</li>
    <li>Readable replies: the share parsed as item lists. An unreadable reply is scored as an empty list.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>An amount estimate must be a positive number of grams. Zero, a missing value or another invalid value
    counts as no amount given. This rule affects amount measures; the food still contributes to identification
    and preparation measures under their usual rules. Supplemental amount measures use the food-code pairs
    described above; photo totals sum amounts regardless of food matching.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Matched foods with an amount: among matched pairs with at least 10 g recorded, the percentage for which
      the model supplied an amount. Pairs are pooled across photos.</li>
    <li>Item grams, median absolute percentage error: for those pairs with an amount, the absolute difference
      between estimated and recorded grams divided by recorded grams. The median is reported as a percentage.</li>
    <li>Photos with an amount: the percentage of scored photos with at least one predicted food given an
      amount, regardless of whether that food matched an ASA24 line.</li>
    <li>Photo grams, median absolute percentage error: sum the positive amounts supplied for each photo, then
      compare that sum with the full ASA24 total. Photos without an amount are omitted. Among the remaining
      photos with at least 10 g recorded, report the median absolute difference divided by recorded grams,
      as a percentage.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A photo total can cover only part of a meal when some foods have no amount. Amount errors therefore
    describe the estimates supplied, while the percentages with amounts describe how often estimates were given.</p>
  <p>The 2023 baselines are rescored from their own published word sets. The additional Nutrition5k comparison
    scores the 507 dishes in the authors' published overhead test list, <code>dish_ids/splits/depth_test_ids.txt</code>,
    using their overhead color photos. Identification uses the weighed ingredient names, restricted to
    ingredients of at least 5 g. Predicted names, listed ingredients and
    reference names are coded with the shared mapper; F1 uses sets of distinct mapped food codes or categories.
    Amount scoring uses the same positive-grams rule. "Dishes with an amount" is the percentage of scored
    dishes with at least one amount supplied. For those dishes, summed estimates are compared with the full
    weighed dish mass; percentage errors require at least 10 g weighed. Ingredients below the 5 g identification
    threshold remain part of that weighed mass.</p>
  <p>Calories and nutrients are neither estimated nor scored. Food identity and amount can be linked separately
    to nutrient values in FNDDS or FoodData Central.</p>

  <h2 id="statistics">7. Intervals and paired comparisons</h2>
  <p>The 95% bootstrap intervals summarize uncertainty from the sampled participants. For per-photo means, the calculation draws
    participants with replacement 1,000 times, keeps all photos of each sampled participant together, and uses
    the 2.5th and 97.5th percentiles of the resulting estimates. Nutrition5k intervals resample dishes.
    Incremental scans made as ingredients were added to a plate are counted as separate dishes, so scans of
    the same plate are treated as independent in these intervals. These
    intervals do not include uncertainty in food records, grading, food coding or model replies.</p>
  <p>Ranks use same-food F1 among graded runs. A rank range covers the middle 95% of ranks obtained after
    resampling participants. The first-place share reports how often a model ranked first; a range of 1 to 1
    does not mean it ranked first in every resample.</p>
  <p>Before-and-after and scale-card comparisons keep the photos and model the same in both runs. Differences
    subtract the standard one-photo result from the changed condition. The participant samples are shared
    across the compared runs. Positive F1 differences favor the changed condition; negative differences in
    corrections or amount error favor it. Amount error is the median absolute log ratio of estimated to
    recorded grams. The difference between two such errors remains on the log-ratio scale.</p>

  <h2 id="limitations">8. Interpretation</h2>
  <p>The grader has not yet been compared with a dietitian's grades. In a repeatability check on 972 food
    lists graded twice, 83% of pairs kept their grade. Means changed by less than one percentage point:
    recorded foods found, for example, was 58.8% and 58.1% in the two runs.</p>
  <p>Individual pairs were less stable than the means. About one pair in six changed grade on the second run,
    with changes about equally frequent in both directions. Per-food labels in the explorer can therefore be wrong.
    Differences between model means of less than one percentage point are within the variation seen in this
    check. Repeatability is not a comparison with a dietitian's judgment.</p>
  <p><code>snapme-eval grader-sheet</code> produces a sheet for a dietitian to grade, and{" "}
    <code>snapme-eval grader-agreement</code> compares the completed grades with the grader's decisions.</p>
  <p>Agreement with ASA24 is agreement with a participant's report. The participants were screened and instructed,
    but foods and portions were not weighed. Most participants were young women; restaurant meals were
    under-represented by design. These data do not represent the full range of people or eating occasions.</p>
  <p>A before photo shows food served, whereas ASA24 records food eaten. Leftovers can therefore contribute to
    differences in grams. Some details in ASA24 descriptions, such as added cooking fat or milk type, may not be
    visible. Broader categories reduce the specificity required for a match, but do not establish which details
    can be inferred from a photo. The photos are public, so prior exposure by a model cannot be ruled out.</p>
</Page>
<style>
  details { margin: 0.5rem 0; max-width: var(--measure); }
  summary { font: 0.92rem var(--sans); cursor: pointer; }
  details pre { white-space: pre-wrap; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
</style>
